WB.L("patterns-in-randomness", "thinking", "intermediate", "Seeing patterns in random noise", "Shuffle a playlist and the same artist plays three times in a row. Rigged? Probably not. Randomness is far lumpier than our brains expect.",
  [
    ["Random looks clumpy", "Real randomness contains streaks and clusters that feel deliberate.", "Ask a class of students to write down a made-up sequence of 100 coin tosses, and ask another group to actually toss a coin 100 times. Teachers who run this classroom trick can usually spot the fakes. Invented sequences switch between heads and tails too often and avoid long runs. Real tosses often include a streak of five or six of the same result, because over 100 tosses such runs are quite likely. Our intuition says random things should look evenly spread out, like sprinkles neatly scattered on a cake. In reality, randomness is clumpy. Rain falls unevenly, stars cluster in the sky, and buses arrive in bunches. When we see a clump, our brains shout 'pattern', even when chance alone would produce it."],
    ["Bombs over London", "Londoners saw a targeting pattern in V-1 strikes; statistics showed chance.", "During the Second World War, German V-1 flying bombs fell on London. Many residents became convinced the strikes were targeted, since some neighbourhoods seemed to be hit repeatedly while others were spared. Some people even moved to apparently safer districts. In 1946, a British actuary named R. D. Clarke published an analysis. He divided a section of south London into hundreds of equal squares and counted the hits in each. The pattern of counts closely matched what you would expect if the bombs had landed at random. The clusters that felt so meaningful were just the natural clumping of chance. The idea of seeing clusters in random data now has a name: the clustering illusion. It still shapes how people interpret crime maps, disease outbreaks and accidents."],
    ["The Texas sharpshooter", "Drawing the target after the shots makes any pattern look impressive.", "Imagine a cowboy firing randomly at the side of a barn. Afterwards, he paints a bullseye around the tightest group of holes and declares himself a sharpshooter. This joke gives its name to the Texas sharpshooter fallacy: finding a cluster first, then treating it as if it had been predicted. It happens whenever people look through lots of data, spot an interesting clump, and forget how many other places they looked. A town might seem to have an unusual number of a particular illness. But there are thousands of towns and many kinds of illness, so somewhere, by chance, there will be a striking cluster. Investigators do take such reports seriously, and some turn out to be real. Many, though, prove to be painted bullseyes."],
    ["The hot hand", "Researchers argued for decades about whether winning streaks are real.", "Basketball fans have long believed in the 'hot hand': a player who has sunk a few shots is more likely to sink the next. In 1985, Thomas Gilovich, Robert Vallone and Amos Tversky analysed shooting records and concluded that streaks were about what chance would produce. The hot hand became a textbook example of seeing patterns in noise. Then, in a paper published in 2018, the economists Joshua Miller and Adam Sanjurjo identified a subtle statistical bias in the way streaks had been measured. Correcting for it, they argued that a modest hot hand effect may exist after all. This is a wonderful twist. It shows that the clustering illusion is real, but also that 'it's just randomness' can itself be a mistake. Careful analysis matters on both sides."],
    ["Faces in the clouds", "Our brains are primed to find meaningful shapes, especially faces.", "In 1976, the Viking 1 spacecraft photographed a region of Mars called Cydonia. One rocky hill, lit by a low Sun, looked startlingly like a human face. Some people claimed it was an artificial monument. Later, sharper images from other spacecraft showed an ordinary eroded mesa. The 'face' had come from shadows, low resolution and our brains. Seeing meaningful images, especially faces, in random shapes is called pareidolia. People find faces in power sockets, burnt toast and the front of cars. It probably reflects how important faces have been to human survival, since recognising friends, enemies and emotions quickly matters. Pareidolia is a lovely quirk. It becomes a problem only when we treat what our pattern detector suggests as if it were evidence."],
    ["Making random feel random", "Truly random can feel rigged, so some designers make things less random.", "When Apple's iPod shuffle feature first appeared, some users complained it wasn't random because it played songs by the same artist close together. Steve Jobs reportedly joked that the company was making shuffle less random to make it feel more random. Whether or not that was the full story, the problem is real and widespread. Music and video apps often adjust their shuffle so that the same artist doesn't come up twice in a row, precisely because genuine randomness feels suspicious. Lottery players show the same instinct. Many avoid numbers like 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 and 6 because they look too orderly, even though that combination is exactly as likely as any other. Our sense of randomness is really a sense of what looks messy."],
    ["Guarding against false patterns", "Decide what you're looking for before you look, and ask how often chance would do it.", "So how do you avoid fooling yourself? First, ask how many chances there were for a pattern to appear. A one in a million coincidence is likely in a country of millions of people. Second, decide what you are testing before you look at the data, rather than searching for something interesting and then explaining it. Scientists do this by registering their plans in advance. Third, check whether the pattern holds up in fresh data. A real effect should show up again; a random clump usually won't. Finally, be humble in both directions. Some patterns are real and important, such as an actual disease cluster or a genuine hot streak. The goal isn't to dismiss every pattern but to test it before you trust it."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "How do invented 'random' coin sequences usually differ from real ones?", ["They have more long streaks", "They switch between heads and tails too often", "They contain only heads", "They are identical to real sequences"], 1, "People avoid long runs, but real randomness often produces them."],
    [1, "What did R. D. Clarke's analysis of V-1 strikes on London show?", ["The bombs targeted rich suburbs", "The hits closely matched a random pattern", "Most bombs missed London entirely", "The bombs followed the Thames"], 1, "The apparent clusters were consistent with bombs landing at random."],
    [2, "What is the Texas sharpshooter fallacy?", ["Shooting at targets that are too far away", "Drawing the target around a cluster after the fact", "Believing guns are always accurate", "Ignoring evidence from Texas"], 1, "Finding a cluster first, then treating it as predicted, makes chance look meaningful."],
    [3, "What did Miller and Sanjurjo's 2018 work suggest about the hot hand?", ["It was proven completely false", "A statistical bias hid a modest hot hand effect", "It only exists in tennis", "It was invented by sports journalists"], 1, "Correcting a subtle measurement bias suggested a small hot hand may be real."],
    [4, "What is pareidolia?", ["Fear of random events", "Seeing meaningful images, like faces, in random shapes", "Remembering numbers in patterns", "A type of lottery strategy"], 1, "Our brains readily find faces and shapes in clouds, toast or Martian hills."]
  ]);

WB.L("regression-to-the-mean", "thinking", "advanced", "Regression to the mean: why extremes don't last", "A footballer has a dream season, then a so-so one. A new treatment seems to cure your worst week. Often the real cause is a quiet statistical force.",
  [
    ["The flight instructors", "Praise seemed to make pilots worse and criticism seemed to make them better.", "The psychologist Daniel Kahneman told a story from his time teaching Israeli air force flight instructors. One instructor objected to the idea that praise works better than punishment. When he praised a trainee for an excellent manoeuvre, he said, the next attempt was usually worse. When he shouted at a trainee for a bad one, the next attempt was usually better. Kahneman realised the instructor was seeing something real but drawing the wrong conclusion. An unusually good flight is partly skill and partly luck, and luck tends not to repeat. So the next attempt is likely to be closer to average, whatever the instructor says. The same goes for unusually bad flights. The praise and shouting were mostly irrelevant. This pattern is called regression to the mean."],
    ["Skill plus luck", "Any result that mixes skill and luck will drift back towards average after an extreme.", "Here is the core idea. Think of any measured performance as skill plus luck. Skill is fairly stable from one day to the next. Luck bounces around randomly. When you pick out an extreme result, the very best or very worst, you are partly selecting for skill and partly selecting for unusually good or bad luck. Next time, the skill is still there, but the luck is likely to be more ordinary. So the result moves back towards the typical level. Crucially, this happens without any cause. Nobody got lazy, discouraged or complacent. It is simply what happens when chance plays a role. The more luck matters in an activity, the stronger the pull back to the average. Pure skill wouldn't regress; pure luck would regress completely."],
    ["Galton's tall families", "The idea was first described by Francis Galton studying the heights of parents and children.", "In the 1880s, the Victorian scientist Francis Galton studied the heights of parents and their adult children. He noticed that very tall parents tended to have children who were tall, but on average not quite as tall as the parents. Very short parents tended to have children who were short, but not quite as short. Galton called this 'regression towards mediocrity', which is where the term comes from. Importantly, this doesn't mean everyone is gradually becoming average height. The spread of heights stays roughly the same from one generation to the next, because some children of average parents end up very tall or very short. Extremes keep appearing; they just aren't always in the same families. It's a reminder that regression describes groups and averages, not destiny."],
    ["The cover jinx", "Many famous 'curses' are just regression in disguise.", "For decades, people have joked about the Sports Illustrated cover jinx: athletes who appear on the American magazine's cover often seem to slump soon after. Similar talk follows players who win big awards or sign large contracts. But think about when someone lands on a magazine cover. It is usually after a remarkable stretch of performance, often their best ever, which almost certainly involved some good luck. The following season is likely to be closer to their usual level, which looks like a slump by comparison. No curse required. The same logic applies to businesses that are praised after a record year, fund managers who topped the charts, and restaurants that have just won an award. Picking people at their peak practically guarantees an apparent decline afterwards."],
    ["Why medicine needs control groups", "People often seek treatment at their worst and improve anyway.", "Think about when you are most likely to try a new remedy for back pain, a headache or a cold. Usually it is when symptoms are at their worst. Many conditions naturally fluctuate, so after a bad patch, things are likely to improve somewhat, whatever you do. If you took a remedy at the peak, it will seem to have worked. This is a major reason medical researchers use control groups. In a well designed trial, some patients get the treatment and others get a placebo or standard care, and both groups are chosen in the same way. Regression affects both groups equally, so only a genuine difference between them shows a real effect. This is general information, so speak to a health professional about your own situation."],
    ["Policies and blackspots", "Fixes aimed at the worst spots often look more effective than they are.", "Imagine a road safety program that installs cameras at the intersections with the most crashes last year. The following year, crashes at those sites fall. Success? Partly, perhaps. But some of those sites were probably at the top of the list because of an unusually unlucky year, and would have improved even with no camera at all. Road safety researchers know about this problem and try to account for it, for example by comparing the treated sites with similar untreated ones or by looking at several years of data rather than one. The same trap appears in schools, where extra help goes to students with the lowest test scores, and in workplaces, where struggling teams get a new manager. Some improvement is real; some is just regression."],
    ["Thinking with regression", "When you select extremes, expect them to move back towards average.", "How can you put this idea to work? First, whenever something is chosen because it was extreme, expect it to look less extreme next time, and don't invent a story to explain the change. Second, when judging an intervention, ask what would have happened without it. Was there a fair comparison group, or just a before and after? Third, predict cautiously. If a new employee has a brilliant first month or a student aces one test, the best forecast for next time sits somewhere between that result and the average. Finally, be careful about lessons drawn from praise and blame. Kahneman's flight instructors were not wrong about what they saw. They were wrong about why. Remembering that luck fades is one of the most useful habits in statistics."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why did the flight instructor's criticism seem to work?", ["Criticism is more effective than praise", "Unusually bad flights were usually followed by more average ones anyway", "Trainees were afraid of the instructor", "The instructor gave better tips when angry"], 1, "Bad luck tends not to repeat, so performance naturally drifted back towards average."],
    [1, "Which kind of activity would show the strongest regression to the mean?", ["One based purely on skill", "One where luck plays a big role", "One with no measured results", "One done only once"], 1, "The more luck contributes to results, the stronger the pull back towards average."],
    [2, "What did Francis Galton notice about very tall parents?", ["Their children were usually even taller", "Their children were tall, but on average less tall than them", "Their children were usually short", "Height wasn't inherited at all"], 1, "Children's heights tended to fall between their parents' heights and the average."],
    [4, "Why do medical trials use control groups?", ["To make trials cheaper", "So regression and other effects hit both groups and only a real difference shows", "Because placebos cure illness", "To test doctors rather than treatments"], 1, "Comparing similar groups separates genuine effects from natural improvement."],
    [5, "Why might crashes fall at new camera sites even without the cameras?", ["Drivers avoid all intersections", "The sites were chosen after unusually bad years that would naturally improve", "Cameras slow down time", "Crash data is always wrong"], 1, "Selecting the worst sites picks up bad luck, which tends to fade."]
  ]);

WB.L("berksons-paradox", "thinking", "advanced", "Berkson's paradox: when filtering creates false patterns", "Why do the best restaurants seem to be in the worst locations, and why do attractive dates seem more likely to be rude? The answer is a sneaky trick of selection.",
  [
    ["A strange hospital pattern", "Looking only at hospital patients can create links between diseases that don't exist outside.", "In 1946, the American statistician Joseph Berkson pointed out a puzzle in medical research. When researchers studied patients in hospital, two unrelated conditions could appear to be linked, either more or less often found together than in the general population. The reason was the way patients got into the data. People are admitted to hospital because they have one condition or another, or both. Looking only at hospital patients means you've filtered the population according to those very conditions. That filtering alone can create a statistical relationship that wouldn't exist in the wider world. This effect is now called Berkson's paradox or Berkson's bias. It is a cousin of selection bias, and once you understand it, you start spotting it in dating, hiring, restaurants and headlines."],
    ["The dating puzzle", "If you only date people who are nice or attractive, those traits will seem to clash.", "The mathematician Jordan Ellenberg popularised a memorable example. Suppose someone will only date a person who is reasonably kind, reasonably attractive, or both. In the general population, imagine kindness and good looks have nothing to do with each other. Now look only at the people this person actually dates. Anyone who is neither kind nor attractive has been filtered out. Among those left, the attractive ones only need to clear one bar, so many of them are not especially kind. The kind ones are often not especially attractive. Within this group, looks and kindness now seem to be opposites. It feels as though attractive people are more likely to be rude. In fact, nothing about the people changed. The filter created the pattern."],
    ["Draw it to see it", "Imagine a scatter plot where one corner has been cut away.", "Picture a square scatter plot full of dots, with talent along one axis and charm along the other. If the two traits are unrelated, the dots fill the square evenly, like a random sprinkle with no tilt. Now imagine a selection rule: only people whose talent plus charm exceeds a certain total are admitted. Draw a diagonal line across the lower left corner and remove every dot beneath it. Look at the dots that remain. Suddenly they slope downwards. Among those who passed, the most talented tend to be less charming and the most charming tend to be less talented. A negative correlation has appeared from nowhere. This is the essence of Berkson's paradox: selecting on a combination of two traits manufactures a relationship between them."],
    ["Restaurants, books and films", "Things that survive often do so because they are strong in at least one way.", "Why does it sometimes seem that the best food is found in obscure, awkward locations? A restaurant can survive in a busy, prime spot even if the food is ordinary, because foot traffic keeps it going. A restaurant in a bad location only survives if the food is excellent. So among restaurants that are still open, location and quality can appear negatively linked. Similar logic applies to books and films. A novel can become widely known because it is brilliantly written or because it has a gripping, marketable plot. Among famous novels, you may notice that the most gripping ones aren't always the best written, and vice versa. The pattern may tell you more about how things became popular than about any real trade off."],
    ["Collider bias in research", "Controlling for the wrong thing can create a false link between a cause and an effect.", "Researchers call the variable at the heart of this problem a 'collider': something influenced by two other factors. Filtering on it, or statistically controlling for it, can create a misleading link between those factors. A well-known puzzle involves birth weight. Babies of mothers who smoke tend to be lighter at birth, and low birth weight is linked to higher risk. Yet among only low birth weight babies, some studies found those born to smokers did slightly better. Smoking wasn't protective. Low birth weight can result from smoking or from other, often more serious problems, so comparing within that group mixed very different situations. During the COVID-19 pandemic, researchers warned that some early studies, which only included tested or hospitalised patients, could be affected by similar collider bias."],
    ["Hiring and admissions", "Among people who passed a selection, the criteria can seem to work against each other.", "Imagine a university that admits students based on a combination of school results and an entrance test. Among admitted students, those with lower school results must have done especially well on the test, and those with lower test scores must have had especially strong school results. So within the student body, the two measures may seem barely related or even negatively related. Someone looking only at current students might conclude that the entrance test predicts nothing. That conclusion could be quite wrong across the full population of applicants. The same trap appears in hiring, where employers sometimes notice that interview performance doesn't seem to predict job success among the people they hired. Part of that may be real, but part may be a filter hiding the relationship."],
    ["Spotting the filter", "Always ask how the people or things in a dataset got there.", "The key defence against Berkson's paradox is a simple question: how did these cases end up in front of me? If the group you're looking at was selected based on the very traits you're comparing, be suspicious of any pattern within it. Your social circle, your newsfeed, a hospital ward, a list of successful companies and the people you have dated are all filtered samples. Patterns within them may be produced by the filter rather than by the world. When possible, look at a broader, unfiltered group. When that isn't possible, be careful about drawing big conclusions. The paradox is a reminder that data never simply speaks for itself. It always arrives through some process, and understanding that process is half the work of understanding the data."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why did Joseph Berkson find misleading links between diseases in hospital patients?", ["Doctors recorded data incorrectly", "Patients were admitted because of those conditions, filtering the sample", "Hospitals cause disease", "The diseases were contagious"], 1, "Selecting patients based on the conditions themselves can create a false relationship."],
    [1, "In the dating example, why do kind and attractive traits seem opposed?", ["Attractive people really are less kind", "People who were neither kind nor attractive were filtered out", "Kindness reduces attractiveness", "Dating apps hide kind people"], 1, "Removing those with neither trait leaves a group where the traits look negatively linked."],
    [2, "What happens to an unrelated scatter plot when you remove the lower left corner?", ["The dots spread evenly", "The remaining dots show a downward slope", "The dots form a circle", "The correlation becomes perfectly positive"], 1, "Selecting on a combined total creates a negative correlation among those who pass."],
    [4, "What is a 'collider' in research?", ["A variable influenced by two other factors", "A type of particle accelerator", "A large randomised trial", "A variable that causes everything"], 0, "Filtering or controlling for a collider can create misleading links between its causes."],
    [6, "What is the best first question to guard against Berkson's paradox?", ["How large is the dataset?", "Who funded the study?", "How did these cases end up in the data?", "Is the result statistically significant?"], 2, "Knowing how the sample was selected reveals whether a filter created the pattern."]
  ]);
